WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.294
GPU driver installation should start with a plan.

00:00:03.294 --> 00:00:08.085
Choose a Linux distribution and kernel strategy that fit the system's purpose.

00:00:08.085 --> 00:00:11.678
Long-term support distributions often favor stability and predictable updates.

00:00:11.678 --> 00:00:15.271
Rolling releases may provide newer kernels and libraries sooner,

00:00:15.271 --> 00:00:18.066
but they can also introduce more change.

00:00:18.066 --> 00:00:22.458
AI systems that support real workflows usually benefit from controlled change.

00:00:22.458 --> 00:00:25.652
Repository packages and vendor packages each have tradeoffs.

00:00:25.652 --> 00:00:30.842
Distribution packages may integrate cleanly with the package manager and kernel update process.

00:00:30.842 --> 00:00:34.435
Vendor packages may provide newer support or specific versions.

00:00:34.435 --> 00:00:40.424
Mixing sources without a plan can create dependency conflicts or unclear ownership of installed files.

00:00:40.424 --> 00:00:42.819
Pick an approach and document it.

00:00:42.819 --> 00:00:46.013
Version control for the stack is operational discipline.

00:00:46.013 --> 00:00:50.405
Record the distribution version, kernel, driver, firmware, CUDA or ROCm components,

00:00:50.405 --> 00:00:53.998
container runtime, inference runtime, Python environment, and relevant libraries.

00:00:53.998 --> 00:00:56.793
Pin versions when the system needs stability.

00:00:56.793 --> 00:01:01.983
Avoid unnecessary upgrades on stable AI systems unless there is a clear reason,

00:01:01.983 --> 00:01:03.979
test plan, and rollback path.

00:01:03.979 --> 00:01:05.975
Build a repeatable installation checklist.

00:01:05.975 --> 00:01:09.569
Include prerequisites, kernel headers, package sources, driver install method,

00:01:09.569 --> 00:01:13.162
Secure Boot considerations, runtime libraries, container configuration, validation commands,

00:01:13.162 --> 00:01:15.957
smoke tests, and backup or snapshot steps.

00:01:15.957 --> 00:01:20.748
A checklist reduces guesswork and makes it possible to rebuild the same

00:01:20.748 --> 00:01:23.143
acceleration stack after a disk failure,

00:01:23.143 --> 00:01:24.740
migration, or failed upgrade.
